---
import bayphl1 from "../../images/1/banner.svg";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <link
      href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/modern-css-reset@1.4.0/dist/reset.min.css"
      rel="stylesheet"
    />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="generator" content={Astro.generator} />
    <title>BayPHL 1</title>
  </head>

  <body>
    <div id="root">
      <div id="header">
        <div class="tabs">
          <a href="#info">Logistics</a>
          <a href="#puzzles">Puzzle Info</a>
          <a href="#register">Registration</a>
          <a href="#contact">Contact</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="banner"><img src={bayphl1.src} alt="BayPHL 1" /></div>

      <div class="block">
        <a href="/"><strong>← Back to BayPHL homepage</strong></a>
      </div>

      <div class="block" id="info">
        <h1>BayPHL 1: Coming November 8th and 9th, 2025</h1>
        <h2>Basic Information</h2>

        <dl>
          <dt>What is this?</dt>
          <dd>
            <p>
              BayPHL is the Bay Area Puzzle Hunt League, a recurring series of
              <a
                href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puzzle_hunt"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer">puzzle hunts</a
              >
              in the Bay Area, inspired by its <a href="https://baphl.org"
                >Boston-area sibling</a
              >.
            </p>
            <p>
              Teams of 4–6 are given challenging puzzles that require various
              logical, lateral-thinking, and creative skills to solve, while
              getting outside and exploring locations in the San Francisco Bay
              Area.
            </p>
          </dd>

          <dt>OK, but what is <em>this</em>, specifically?</dt>
          <dd>
            <p>
              BayPHL 1 is an adaptation of the recent <a
                href="https://24.baphl.org"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer">BAPHL 24</a
              >, which ran in August in Watertown, MA. We’ve taken the puzzles
              and route from that event and re-imagined them for the Bay Area.
              The overall experience will be very similar, although we’ve made
              some adjustments in cases where puzzles depended on specific
              physical elements unique to the Watertown route.
            </p>

            <p>
              (You will have an advantage though, as it probably won’t be 90°F
              outside this time!)
            </p>
          </dd>

          <dt>When is BayPHL 1 happening?</dt>
          <dd>
            <p>
              <strong
                >BayPHL 1 will have two separate identical instances</strong
              >
              — Saturday, November 8, 2025, and Sunday, November 9, 2025, both starting
              at 9:30 AM and running until <em>at least</em> 5:30 PM (but based on
              our experiences in Boston, teams should expect to stick around for
              a bit longer). Teams can participate on either Saturday or Sunday.
            </p>

            <p>
              The route will be entirely outdoors, and will start and end near
              <a
                href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/KVzf2RPTEaS2AFRA6"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer">Ghiradelli Square in San Francisco</a
              >. Solvers should expect to walk around 4.5 miles over the course
              of the route. More specifics will be given to registered teams in
              the week leading up to the event.
            </p>
          </dd>

          <dt>How do I register?</dt>
          <dd>
            Registration will open on
            <span data-tippy-timestamp="1757980800"
              >Monday, September 15th at 5PM Pacific</span
            >. There will be options to register a team, or you can sign up as
            an unattached hunter, and we will do our best to match you with
            other solvers. Ahead of that, we highly recommend checking in with
            your friends and figuring out who your team will be and your
            preference of day for solving — we will ask you for your date
            preferences during registration, and have a maximum number of teams
            we can support each day, on a first-come-first-serve basis.
          </dd>

          <dt>How much will it cost?</dt>
          <dd>
            BayPHL 1 will cost $40 for a team to participate. BayPHL is a fully
            volunteer written and run event, so all of your registration costs
            go toward puzzle production and other overhead.
          </dd>

          <dt>What is your refund policy?</dt>
          <dd>
            <p>
              If you register and pay for BayPHL 1 and then find that you are
              unable to make the event, we will do our best to replace your team
              with another team from the waitlist. If we are able to do so, we
              would be happy to refund your registration fee. However, if we are
              unable to do so, we will not be able to issue a refund.
            </p>
          </dd>

          <dt>What if it rains?</dt>
          <dd>
            <p>
              We’ll get wet, and we’ll all be a little sad. If it gets really
              bad, we’ll do our best to adjust, but you should be prepared for
              whatever nature will bring.
            </p>

            <p>
              (Even if it doesn’t rain, it will likely be windy and chilly, so
              dress in layers!)
            </p>
          </dd>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div class="block" id="puzzles">
        <h2>Puzzle Info</h2>

        <dl>
          <dt>What kinds of puzzles will there be?</dt>
          <dd>
            Every hunt is a little bit different. If you want to get a sense of
            the types of puzzles there could be, you can check out some examples
            from prior BAPHL events:
            <a
              href="https://baphl.org/23/#puzzles"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer">BAPHL 23</a
            >,
            <a
              href="https://22.baphl.org/#puzzles"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer">BAPHL 22</a
            >, or
            <a
              href="https://www.baphl.org/21/#puzzles"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer">BAPHL 21</a
            >.
          </dd>

          <dt>How hard is the hunt?</dt>
          <dd>
            <p>
              BayPHL 1 will consist of approximately 20 puzzles of potentially
              wildly varying lengths. Puzzle difficulty is pretty hard to
              convey, so we’ll lean on comparison. We think this hunt is similar
              in difficulty to past BAPHLs, if you’re familiar with any of them.
              It’s probably easier than most MIT Mystery Hunt puzzles, but
              harder than Puzzled Pint. It’s probably a little harder than DASH
              (or, if you participated in them, <a
                href="https://bayareanightgame.org/"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                target="_blank">BANGs</a
              >).
            </p>

            <p>
              More importantly, it’s definitely longer than Puzzled Pint or
              DASH, if those are your comparison points. As you can see on the <a
                href="https://24.baphl.org"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer">BAPHL 24</a
              > website, we only had 16 out of 50 teams finish, and even many of
              those didn’t finish within the first 8 hours. We recommend planning
              to spend all day on the hunt, through dinner. Our ending location has
              some great hangout spots, so there will be opportunities to sit and
              socialize with other participants if you finish early. And if finishing
              is important to you, we will have volunteers available all day who
              are eager to provide as much or as little help as required or desired
              for you to accomplish your goals.
            </p>
          </dd>

          <dt>What kind of supplies will we need?</dt>
          <dd>
            <p>
              You’ll want lots of solving supplies — paper, pencils, scissors,
              tape, highlighters, markers, rulers, etc. While there’s lots of
              open space at all of our locations, there’s not always an
              abundance of seating, so we strongly recommend bringing clipboards
              and either a picnic blanket or some lightweight camp chairs.
            </p>

            <p>
              You’ll also want to dress for comfort while walking and solving
              outside. In November in the Marina District, plan for anything
              from warm and sunny to cold and foggy to damp and rainy, but
              remember that San Francisco’s microclimates mean that the weather
              on our route may not match the weather near you.
            </p>
          </dd>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div class="block" id="register">
        <h2>BayPHL 1 Registration</h2>

        <p>
          Registration will open on
          <span data-tippy-timestamp="1757980800"
            >Monday, September 15th at 5PM Pacific</span
          >
          (check back here at that time). There will be options to register a team,
          or to sign up as an unattached hunter and we will do our best to match
          you with other solvers. Ahead of that, we highly recommend checking in
          with your friends and figuring out who your team will be and your preference
          of day for solving — we will ask you for your date preferences during registration,
          and have a maximum number of teams we can support each day, on a first-come-first-serve
          basis.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="block" id="contact">
        Questions? Comments? Please reach out to us at
        <a href="mailto:bayphl1@bayphl.org">bayphl1@bayphl.org</a>.
      </div>
    </div>

    <style is:global>
      :root {
        --color-rust: #a0522d;
        --color-cream: #f5f5dc;
        --color-sand: #f4e4c1;
        --color-espresso: #3c2414;
        --color-slate-300: #2b3d4f;
        --color-slate-500: #486684;
        --color-sage-100: #1f2519;
        --color-sage-200: #3f4b32;
        --color-sage-300: #5e704b;
        --color-sage-400: #7d9564;
        --color-sage-500: #9caf88;
        --color-sage-600: #b0bfa0;
        --color-sage-700: #c4cfb7;
        --color-sage-800: #d7dfcf;
        --color-sage-900: #ebefe7;

        font-family: "Inter", sans-serif;

        --color-background: var(--color-sand);
        --color-surface: var(--color-cream);
        --color-text: var(--color-espresso);
        --color-border: var(--color-rust);
        --color-link: var(--color-slate-500);
        --color-link-hover: var(--color-slate-300);
        --color-tab-hover: var(--color-sage-700);
      }

      @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
        :root {
          --color-background: var(--color-espresso);
          --color-surface: var(--color-rust);
          --color-text: var(--color-sand);
          --color-border: #8b6f42; /* Warmer brown border instead of slate */
          --color-link: var(--color-sage-700);
          --color-link-hover: var(--color-sage-500);
          --color-tab-hover: var(--color-sage-200);
        }
      }

      html,
      body {
        margin: 0;
        padding: 0;
      }

      body {
        background: var(--color-background);
        color: var(--color-text);
        min-height: 100vh;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
      }
    </style>

    <style>
      @import url("https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:ital,opsz,wght@0,14..32,100..900;1,14..32,100..900&family=Roboto+Slab:wght@100..900&display=swap");

      h1,
      h2,
      h3,
      h4,
      h5,
      h6,
      .tabs a {
        font-family: "Roboto Slab", serif;
        font-weight: bold;
        color: var(--color-text);
      }

      h1 {
        font-weight: 900;
      }

      h2 {
        border-bottom: 1px solid #000000;
        margin-bottom: 0.5em;
      }

      p {
        margin-bottom: 1rem;

        &:last-child {
          margin-bottom: 0;
        }
      }

      a {
        color: var(--color-link);
      }

      a:hover {
        color: var(--color-link-hover);
      }

      dt {
        font-weight: bold;
      }
      dd {
        margin-bottom: 5px;
        margin-left: 25px;
        margin-top: 5px;
      }

      p,
      dd {
        line-height: 1.25;
      }

      #root {
        max-width: 980px;
        min-height: 100vh;
        margin-inline: auto;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        padding-top: 20px;
      }

      #header {
        padding-bottom: 8px;
      }

      #banner {
        width: 100%;
        max-width: 690px;
        margin: 20px auto;
      }

      .block {
        margin-bottom: 8px;
        margin-top: 0px;
        background: var(--color-surface);
        border: 2px solid var(--color-border);
        padding: 15px;
      }

      .tabs {
        display: flex;
        gap: 0.5ex;
        justify-content: end;
        flex-wrap: wrap;

        > * {
          border: 2px solid var(--color-border);
          padding: 5px 10px;
          background: var(--color-surface);
        }

        a {
          text-decoration: none;
        }

        a:hover {
          background-color: var(--color-tab-hover);
        }
      }

      [data-tippy-timestamp] {
        text-decoration: underline dotted;
        cursor: help;
      }
    </style>
    <script>
      import tippy from "tippy.js";
      import "tippy.js/dist/tippy.css";
      tippy("[data-tippy-timestamp]", {
        placement: "top",
        theme: "light",
        content: function (el) {
          const timestamp = parseInt(
            el.getAttribute("data-tippy-timestamp") ?? "0"
          );
          const date = new Date(timestamp * 1000);
          return date.toLocaleString("en-US", {
            month: "long",
            day: "numeric",
            year: "numeric",
            hour: "numeric",
            minute: "2-digit",
            timeZoneName: "short",
          });
        },
      });
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
